From 78b65b14317ba680b3c0db6472d44e9a5dcbe289 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: claude <noreply@anthropic.com>
Date: Sun, 23 Aug 2026 12:54:33 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] test(live): prove all 74 images render and serve, not one of
 them
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=UTF-8
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

verify-live.sh is the only check here that can see a delivery failure, and it
fetched exactly one rendered image before printing "live and serving". The
manifest declares 74 slots and the sitemap publishes 154 image URLs to Google.
Proving one is the comfort that let F-131 ship — 28 diagrams committed,
imported, and 404ing in production while every source-reading guard passed — and
F-173, one image bundled into every visitor's JavaScript, sitemapped, and
rendered by no page.

verify-live-images.mjs sweeps all 74. It builds the slot-to-page map from the
manifest's own HREFS, fetches each of the 54 pages once, and asserts two
separate facts per slot: the bundled filename appears in the HTML that came
back, and that URL returns real bytes. Presence without delivery is a broken
image; delivery without presence is an image nobody sees. Both have shipped
here, and neither was visible from source.

The matcher self-tests before it is trusted — six assertions, no network,
including that an id which merely prefixes another does not match. A guard that
cannot fail is not a guard.

Separately: fourteen public pages rendered no image at all. Four are indexes
listing items that already owned artwork the index never displayed — /papers and
/guides show thirteen items between them, every one with a published image,
visible only after the click. IllustrationThumb fixes that with zero new art. It
is not <Illustration> on purpose: on an index the picture is wayfinding beside a
link that already carries the words, so it is decorative by construction rather
than a figure whose alt a screen reader would read immediately before the title
saying the same thing.

The remaining ten are recorded as tier 6, with /service-areas/[city] — 32 live
local-search pages — named as the largest gap, and four pages recorded as
correctly imageless so nobody adds one later.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
